---
// Recognition section for professionals — the pain points, in order of how
// often practitioners say them out loud: insight that doesn't move anything,
// the session clock, the pre-verbal ceiling, the moment real emotion arrives,
// and what stays in the practitioner's own body. The accent quote is Jacob's
// own (copy book §2); the pull line is copy book B.12.
---

<section class="we-opening">
  <div class="we-opening-inner">
    <div class="we-eyebrow">You know this moment</div>
    <h2 class="we-opening-head">
      They understand it perfectly —<br />
      and still <em>nothing moves.</em>
    </h2>
    <p class="we-opening-body">
      If you work with people — in a practice, a classroom, a session room, a
      team — you know the moment. The pattern has been named. The story has
      been told twice, and in talking, people still control what they say —
      whole hours can go into hiding. The insight is accurate, and it changes
      nothing. The hour ends where it ended last time: at the edge of
      something neither of you could quite reach.
    </p>
    <p class="we-opening-body">
      You know the other versions too. The mixed feeling that takes ten
      minutes to set up in words — words go single file — while the clock
      runs. The oldest material, from before language, that no conversation
      can reach: anyone working near attachment or the earliest years knows
      that ceiling. And the moment nobody mentions at conferences: real
      emotion finally arrives in the room — the flood, or the shutdown — and
      the honest inner question is <em>what do I do now?</em>
    </p>
    <p class="we-opening-body">
      Then there's the quieter version, the one about you. You hold other
      people's hardest hours all week, and some of what they bring stays in
      your body after the door closes. Most professionals have no practice
      where that ever gets heard.
    </p>
    <p class="we-opening-body">
      This masterclass is about exactly those moments. In Somatic Vocal
      Healing the question isn't <em>tell me more about it</em> — it's
      <em>what is the sound of it?</em> Sound before story: a feeling that
      would take ten careful minutes to circle in words arrives whole, in one
      honest tone — and the person in front of you was born knowing how to
      make it.
    </p>
    <p class="we-opening-body">
      It doesn't replace what you do. It goes underneath it — a way to let
      the body speak when the mind has nowhere left to go.
    </p>
    <p class="we-opening-accent">
      "I've never had a therapist ask me: <em>could you make a sound for
      that?</em> It's odd and sad that it doesn't happen — because it's still
      so natural to us." — Jacob
    </p>
    <p class="we-opening-pull">
      Talking circles it.<br />Sounding enters it.
    </p>
